Traumatic brain injury
Acute Urgent Care
Altered GCS post-trauma, Loss of consciousness/Amnesia, Pupillary abnormalities, Intracranial haemorrhage on CT

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AUC-051
Reason for Visit: Severe head trauma with loss of consciousness, resulting in ongoing confusion, repetitive vomiting, and unequal pupils.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and roommate
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a patient who fell backward off a wall, struck the back of their head on concrete, and lost consciousness for approximately 2 minutes. The patient is now severely confused, vomiting repeatedly, and the roommate noted one pupil looks larger than the other. This clinical picture is highly alarming for an expanding acute intracranial haemorrhage. Immediate, on-site assessment was mandated to evaluate the Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS) and immediately trigger a Priority 1 trauma transport, as any delay guarantees progressive, fatal brain herniation before routine clinic access.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: [Roommate reports] He fell and hit his head really hard, was knocked out cold, and now he keeps throwing up and asking the same questions over and over.
History of Present Illness: The patient sustained a blunt-force head injury 30 minutes ago after falling approximately 1.5 meters backward off a retaining wall, impacting the occiput on a concrete surface. The roommate witnessed a transient loss of consciousness at the time of injury lasting roughly 2 minutes. Upon awakening, the patient exhibits significant post-traumatic amnesia, repeatedly asking what happened. The patient complains of a severe, localized occipital headache that is progressively worsening. Over the last 15 minutes, there has been profound nausea accompanied by three episodes of recurrent, forceful vomiting.
Functional Status: Lying on the couch, highly lethargic and confused, unable to sit up without severe dizziness/vomiting.
Social/Safety Concerns: Imminent risk of severe neurological decline and airway compromise from vomiting while confused.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 165/95 | HR: 55 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 14 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Patient appears drowsy, pale, and is holding the back of his head; actively retching into a bucket.
Physical Exam: Neurological examination reveals an altered level of consciousness with a Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS) of 13 (E3, V4, M6). Pupillary examination identifies significant abnormalities: anisocoria is present, with the right pupil sluggish and 2mm larger than the left, indicating early uncal herniation/third nerve compression. Gross motor function is currently symmetrical without obvious hemiparesis. Inspection of the head reveals a large, boggy occipital haematoma. There are currently no overt signs of basilar skull fracture (e.g., bilateral periorbital ecchymosis), though this remains a risk. Cardiovascular exam reveals hypertension with relative bradycardia, highly suspicious for early Cushing's reflex driven by rising intracranial pressure. Expected investigation results include an acute intracranial haemorrhage, midline shift, or severe cerebral oedema visible on a non-contrast CT scan of the head.
Environmental Observations: Mechanism of injury location (concrete wall) verified.
Medication Reconciliation: No known regular medications. No anticoagulants.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Traumatic brain injury - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Simple Concussion (Mild TBI) - Ruled out/unlikely due to the presence of objective red flags: recurrent forceful vomiting, an abnormal pupillary exam (anisocoria), and a declining GCS, which collectively define a moderate-to-severe injury with likely structural damage.

Alcohol Intoxication - Ruled out/unlikely as the primary cause of the focal neurological signs (sluggish unequal pupil) and the clear temporal relationship to a high-impact mechanical trauma, though co-ingestion can complicate assessment.

Epidural Haematoma - A specific subtype of TBI; highly likely given the classic "lucid interval" followed by rapid deterioration and vomiting, requiring immediate CT imaging to differentiate from subdural or parenchymal bleeds.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with the diagnostic tetrad for a severe traumatic brain injury: altered GCS post-trauma, documented loss of consciousness with amnesia, pupillary abnormalities, and high clinical suspicion for an intracranial haemorrhage based on vital signs (Cushing's reflex). The expanding intracranial bleed is increasing pressure on the brainstem, driving the repetitive vomiting and the sluggish pupil. An urgent after-hours assessment was unequivocally life-saving; identifying the anisocoria and Cushing's reflex mandated the immediate activation of an Intensive Care Paramedic response for airway protection and rapid transport to a neurosurgical center. Delaying this assessment would have resulted in fatal brainstem herniation.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Positioned the patient in a semi-recumbent position (head elevated 30 degrees) to facilitate venous drainage and lower intracranial pressure, while maintaining strict cervical spine precautions manually. Immediately called 000 for Priority 1 Intensive Care Paramedic dispatch, explicitly declaring a "declining head injury with anisocoria" to trigger a neurosurgical trauma response. Administered Ondansetron 4mg ODT to suppress vomiting and reduce spikes in intracranial pressure.
Positive Behavioral Change: (N/A - Focus is entirely on acute trauma stabilization and rapid extrication).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Helicopter Emergency Medical Service (HEMS) or rapid road transport to a major trauma center.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Handover to paramedics and neurosurgical trauma team.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Ambulance dispatched. The roommate must maintain continuous observation of the patient's airway and consciousness. If the patient completely loses consciousness and begins to vomit, they must be log-rolled as a single unit into the lateral position to prevent fatal aspiration.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The severe impact has likely caused bleeding inside your skull, which is putting dangerous pressure on your brain. You require an emergency CT scan and potentially neurosurgery.
Clinical Course: The hospital team will scan your head immediately upon arrival. If there is a large, expanding clot pressing on the brain, the neurosurgeons will take you straight to the operating room to drill a small hole or remove a piece of skull to release the pressure and stop the bleeding.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to sit up or walk. The effort will increase the pressure inside your head. We are keeping you propped up slightly to help the blood drain from your brain while we wait.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your brain is enclosed in a rigid bone box. The fall has caused bleeding inside this box, leaving the blood nowhere to go but to press against your brain tissue. This pressure is causing the unequal pupils and the severe vomiting. The paramedics will arrive shortly to manage your breathing and transport you safely.
Actionable Steps: Let your roommate answer my questions so you can rest your brain. Try to keep your eyes closed if the light makes the headache worse.

